I ruled on finite-funding-2 and committed the package change as f45e47eb.

**Ruling:** `LEGACY_UNCAPPED_DOLLARS` becomes 10_000_000_000_000 (ten trillion dollars a year), up from 100 billion.
- **Why it can't bind:** that amount pays the federal part ($443,108 a home) of about 22.6 million affordable homes a year. That is more than fifty times all the homes in San Francisco, so no policy on a San Francisco dataset reaches it. The spec's "such a policy plays as it did" now rests on that limit. It is also stated as the one case where the pool would bind: a year whose federal asks pass the amount.
- **"No ceiling":** the new amount is above `MAX_DOLLARS` (100 billion, the most an added pool's inflow may hold). So "No ceiling" shows only on an older dataset's federal pool, never on a pool a player adds.
- **Exact sums:** even at the largest scale an editor offers (1,000%), 480 months of money stay below 2^53 dollars, so totals stay exact whole numbers.
- **Tests:** they use the constant instead of literal dollar amounts. The synthetic-city "unlimited money" test in `engine-finance.spec.ts` keeps a larger amount of its own, with a comment saying why. The older-fixture check now reads "never falls below 99% of `LEGACY_UNCAPPED_DOLLARS`" instead of "$99 billion".

I rejected the other two options:
- **Option 1** needs a check on the live dataset that no one can run here.
- **Option 3** would let some older published policies play differently, against D5 and the rule that every policy published before this build keeps working.

The F2 interface row changed, and its copies in the design doc and the finite-funding, funding-measures and pool-closing specs match it. All three units get a clarification, with no work cut back: finite-funding changes the constant in Task 3 plus the tests and docs that use it.

